WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.767 --> 00:00:03.336
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated comedian

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:05.805
who will be returning as
guest host of "The Daily Show"

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:07.706
next week on Comedy Central,
and her memoir,

00:00:07.706 --> 00:00:09.708
"Leslie [Bleep] Jones,"
is on sale now.

00:00:09.708 --> 00:00:11.778
Please welcome back to the show,
our good friend,

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:13.713
Leslie [Bleep] Jones, everybody!

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:16.716
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:43.609 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:45.979
Welcome back, my friend.

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:47.180
[ Cheering ]

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.048
Yeah.

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:50.216
[ Cheering ]

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:52.719
If you got it, you got it.
Flaunt it.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:55.655
-My stylist said, "Make sure
that you show the leg, bitch."

00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:56.890
-Yeah.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.925
[ Cheering ]

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.593
-I've been lucky enough
to talk to you

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:01.994
about this book
a couple of times now.

00:01:01.994 --> 00:01:05.431
You did a wonderful book event
in New York City.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:07.066
You came on a podcast
with me and my brother

00:01:07.066 --> 00:01:08.968
and can I say --
-You're so sweet.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:11.704
-Oh, I love you.
-Seth loves me!

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:13.606
-I will say,
we did a book event.

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:14.473
-We should have a baby.

00:01:14.473 --> 00:01:16.776
-What?
-Nothing.

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:20.145
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.145 --> 00:01:21.381
-[ Sighs ]

00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:22.782
-Did you see how he reacted?

00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:24.383
Like we had really did
something.

00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:27.587
Like, "You pregnant?"

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:29.022
-I mean,
the book event went well.

00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:31.724
I don't remember going
that well.

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:33.125
I would say, it was

00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:35.261
a great symphony space
on the Upper West Side,

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:36.596
and I feel like
a lot of those people

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:38.030
go to book events
on the Upper West Side,

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.232
and I think it was
they've never heard answers

00:01:40.232 --> 00:01:42.835
about a book that you gave
at that book event.

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:43.936
-I loved it so much.

00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:45.838
-Yeah?
-You had no control over me.

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:49.275
-No, I almost never do.

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:51.243
Now, did your editors have
control over you in your book?

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:52.912
-No. Absolutely not.

00:01:52.912 --> 00:01:54.514
Absolutely not.
They kept asking for it.

00:01:54.514 --> 00:01:55.781
They were like,
"Are you finished?"

00:01:55.781 --> 00:01:58.051
No, I'm not finished.

00:01:58.051 --> 00:02:00.453
-Did they fight you on the title
or was the title...?

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.355
-They fought me on everything.
-Yeah.

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:04.090
-Look, okay, there's a deadline.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.693
There's a deadline and I --
yeah, blew the deadline.

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:08.093
Yeah.

00:02:08.093 --> 00:02:11.164
I reread the whole book
and rewrote the whole book,

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:15.034
and they was like,
"You can't do that."

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:19.104
It's my book. I can do whatever
the [bleep] I want to!

00:02:19.104 --> 00:02:21.274
[ Cheering ]
-I want to know --

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:23.643
I've heard people --
You did something, though

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.311
I have not heard
anyone say they've done before

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:27.280
when you talk about,
you can't be controlled,

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.914
even you couldn't control you,

00:02:28.914 --> 00:02:31.617
because you, when you went in
to do the audiobook,

00:02:31.617 --> 00:02:33.486
you admitted you wouldn't
even read it

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:35.888
the way you wrote it.
-Okay.

00:02:35.888 --> 00:02:37.723
-It went just like this.

00:02:37.723 --> 00:02:39.425
It went just like this.

00:02:43.262 --> 00:02:45.397
"Foreword.

00:02:45.397 --> 00:02:47.099
When Leslie --" No.

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:52.705
First, what had happened was --

00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:54.040
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.408
-And the editor was like,
"I made her take out

00:02:56.408 --> 00:02:57.710
all the
what had happened wases."

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.346
-Yo, yo, the director was like,

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:01.614
"Um, I don't know, maybe we --"

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:04.317
I was like, "Girl,
this is how it's going, girl.

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.518
I'm not finna sit here
and read this book and be like,

00:03:06.518 --> 00:03:09.789
'Oh, Leslie Jones,
she said that she --'

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:11.490
But since a cop had --'"

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:15.128
No, I know the story,

00:03:15.128 --> 00:03:16.563
and I'm finna
give you the details.

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:18.798
I'm going to give you
the real tea.

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:21.000
They didn't put that in there.
They was trying to hide it.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.334
Let me tell you.

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:25.237
I think it's the rarest case
where, you know, like,

00:03:25.237 --> 00:03:27.373
sometimes when I'll be --
I'll like, read a book

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:30.910
on like, my Kindle and then
I'll switch over to Audible.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.712
When you go back, it's like,
"you were on this page."

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:36.181
I feel like with your book,
it'd be like, "We don't know.

00:03:36.181 --> 00:03:37.783
We don't know where you are.
We don't know what to tell you."

00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:40.086
-I was like, "Just ride with me.
Tell me if I go too deep."

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.088
And there was some stories
I would go so deep in it,

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:44.523
she'd be like, "Um, Leslie,

00:03:44.523 --> 00:03:50.062
we're -- we're only in
Chapter 2 and we're over.

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:51.497
It's over, that whole chapter."

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:54.033
And I was like,
"No, I got more to say."

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:56.135
So I thought it was
only 7 hours.

00:03:56.135 --> 00:04:00.039
I come to find out
it's 16 hours long.

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:03.743
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:08.281
-It's the "War and Peace"
of audio memoirs.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:11.584
Now, I want to get --
real talk here.

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:13.485
You actually wrote this memoir,
and you had to go back

00:04:13.485 --> 00:04:14.920
and you had to remember things,

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.389
and you maybe had to revisit
tougher times in your life.

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:20.092
Was it cathartic
to go back to those things?

00:04:20.092 --> 00:04:22.594
Was it difficult, ever,
to go back to the past?

00:04:22.594 --> 00:04:24.998
-Oh, of course,
because now you look and I go,

00:04:24.998 --> 00:04:26.432
"Oh, my God,
I made it through that?

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:27.933
Oh, my God, how did I not die?

00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:30.470
Oh, my God,
why would I say that?

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:31.570
Oh, my God!"

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:32.672
It was a lot of stuff in there,

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:34.006
I was like,
"Girl, you can't say that.

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:35.074
Oh, you have to take that out."

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:36.075
And then there was
a lot of things --

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.176
because, you know,
I smoke weed,

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:38.377
so I don't remember
a lot of stuff

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:40.145
and oh, my God.

00:04:40.145 --> 00:04:41.213
You know Dina, right?

00:04:41.213 --> 00:04:43.382
You know
her last name was Moles?

00:04:43.382 --> 00:04:44.516
-Uh-huh, Dina Moles, yeah.

00:04:44.516 --> 00:04:46.685
-I call her Morales.
Her new name is Morales.

00:04:46.685 --> 00:04:49.355
-Okay, so you called her
he wrong name in the book?

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:50.623
-There's so many things.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:54.460
So my coach hit me,
and he was like, "Yeah,

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.128
that's that's not
exactly how I went."

00:04:56.128 --> 00:04:57.696
I was like,
"It's how I remembered it."

00:04:57.696 --> 00:04:59.298
That's how I started saying it.

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:03.202
I was like, "Well,
this is the story I remember."

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:06.439
No, it's really pretty accurate.

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:08.107
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:10.676
-You talk about some people --

00:05:10.676 --> 00:05:12.644
Look, you came across, you know,

00:05:12.644 --> 00:05:14.880
you broke into "SNL"
later in your career

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.449
than maybe other people,
but you met

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:18.717
a lot of very successful
people over your path,

00:05:18.717 --> 00:05:20.219
and some of them
gave you a lot of advice.

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:23.189
Some told you about the value
of getting life experience

00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:25.224
as well, outside of
just getting onstage.

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:27.326
What were some of the things
you did that were helpful?

00:05:27.326 --> 00:05:30.095
-Yeah, I just went
to a lot of jobs.

00:05:30.095 --> 00:05:33.332
I had a lot of jobs,
a lot of jobs.

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:37.804
Yeah, I got fired a lot.
-Yeah.

00:05:37.804 --> 00:05:39.538
I -- yeah --

00:05:39.538 --> 00:05:40.605
Did I ever I quit?

00:05:40.605 --> 00:05:42.375
I quit, but yeah. No.

00:05:42.375 --> 00:05:43.776
-Did you do a lot of, like,

00:05:43.776 --> 00:05:45.444
"You can't fire me, I quit,"
because that doesn't count.

00:05:45.444 --> 00:05:47.579
-No, I was
never in that situation.

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:49.515
It was always like,
"You can go."

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:52.485
Like, "We're going
to let you go now."

00:05:52.485 --> 00:05:54.853
It was always like,
"Oh, we can't keep you on."

00:05:54.853 --> 00:05:56.889
"Uh,
going a different direction."

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:58.724
Or, "Just get out."

00:05:58.724 --> 00:06:00.259
Like, you know?

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:01.760
-Looking back,

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.129
do you think in most
of those cases

00:06:03.129 --> 00:06:04.296
they were right to
send you home?

00:06:04.296 --> 00:06:05.731
-Yes, absolutely.

00:06:05.731 --> 00:06:07.967
Oh, my God, one job,
I used to --

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:10.636
I thought I was going
to be a receptionist.

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:13.005
[ Laughing loudly ]

00:06:16.742 --> 00:06:18.611
It's hilarious, right?

00:06:18.611 --> 00:06:21.313
[ Laughs ] I couldn't
even answer the phone.

00:06:21.313 --> 00:06:22.715
I think it was
a construction company.

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:24.116
I'd be like,
"Alpha Construction."

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:25.651
And the girl was like,
"You got to be more..."

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:28.654
I was like,
"Alpha Construction."

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:31.457
And they would come to --
Man, I would go to sleep.

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:34.193
I would go to sleep at the desk.

00:06:34.193 --> 00:06:36.095
[ Laughter ]

00:06:36.095 --> 00:06:37.963
Because I did comedy
the night before,

00:06:37.963 --> 00:06:39.632
and I was like,
"Man, I'm finna take a nap."

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:43.302
"You are the receptionist.
You can't take a nap."

00:06:43.302 --> 00:06:46.572
So I won a contest
and I came in the next day

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:48.741
and I was bragging, like,
"I won this contest.

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:50.475
I won $1,000 in this contest."

00:06:50.475 --> 00:06:51.744
And they was like, "Oh, great."

00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:52.912
And the girl called me
in the office.

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:54.447
She was like, you know,
"I'm so glad

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:58.317
that the comedy is going well...

00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:02.020
because we're going to have
to let you go."

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:03.789
-When she heard
you won a contest,

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:05.757
she was like,
"Today is the day."

00:07:05.757 --> 00:07:07.460
-Today is the perfect day.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:08.494
-The window opened a little bit.

00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:09.795
-Oh, I clowned, too.

00:07:09.795 --> 00:07:12.865
-I was like, "Oh, bitch,
I'm finna be famous!"

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:18.537
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:18.537 --> 00:07:20.672
-You should go back
with all the money you have now.

00:07:20.672 --> 00:07:22.908
You should buy that desk
and use it to take naps on now.

00:07:22.908 --> 00:07:24.944
-They ain't open no more.
-They ain't open no more?

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:27.747
Well, yeah. Nobody there
to answer the phones.

00:07:27.747 --> 00:07:28.981
Hey, this is very exciting.

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:31.984
Next week,
you were the first guest host

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:33.285
when "The Daily Show"
started doing this

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:35.020
and you get to go back,
do a second week.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:38.690
You were fantastic, fantastic
the first time.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:40.059
[ Cheering ]
-Yes.

00:07:40.059 --> 00:07:42.194
Are you excited? And my other,
just quick follow-up --

00:07:42.194 --> 00:07:44.196
do you feel like you understand
it a little bit more

00:07:44.196 --> 00:07:45.664
going into it now,
having done a week?

00:07:45.664 --> 00:07:50.068
-Oh first of all, it's so ran
like a well-oiled machine.

00:07:50.068 --> 00:07:52.371
-They know what they're doing.
-They'd make anybody look good.

00:07:52.371 --> 00:07:55.474
I'm going to be honest with you.
They make anybody look good.

00:07:55.474 --> 00:07:59.011
Their writers over there
are just like, big brains.

00:07:59.011 --> 00:08:00.812
They're just --

00:08:00.812 --> 00:08:02.915
They just, like,
some of the stuff?

00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:04.850
I love it.
I love it over there.

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:06.785
And I love to
be able to express,

00:08:06.785 --> 00:08:09.521
because I feel like I'm just
like the people's people.

00:08:09.521 --> 00:08:11.056
I understand
what they understand.

00:08:11.056 --> 00:08:13.626
Like, I don't know
if you saw that

00:08:13.626 --> 00:08:15.595
that I thought that
the Electoral College

00:08:15.595 --> 00:08:17.496
was where the politicians
go to college.

00:08:17.496 --> 00:08:18.830
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:21.000
-And they learn college stuff,
you know what I mean?

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.269
They learn, you know,
it's a whole class.

00:08:23.269 --> 00:08:25.103
So I was like,
how you gonna know

00:08:25.103 --> 00:08:26.438
what the Constitution say?

00:08:26.438 --> 00:08:30.809
So, you know, I was explained
that that's not right.

00:08:30.809 --> 00:08:33.245
-Yeah.
-So it's like, stuff like that.

00:08:33.245 --> 00:08:37.583
It's a lot of stuff that
people are not informed about.

00:08:37.583 --> 00:08:40.553
And I feel like you
get to watch MSNBC

00:08:40.553 --> 00:08:42.188
and CNN and get all of that.

00:08:42.188 --> 00:08:44.056
Now come here so I can
make you laugh about it.

00:08:44.056 --> 00:08:47.960
That's what I want,
to make people laugh.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:51.797
You guys need to laugh, man.
Y'all not laughing.

00:08:51.797 --> 00:08:53.732
[ Applause ]
Y'all just so sad.

00:08:53.732 --> 00:08:57.069
Everybody's so sad right now.

00:08:57.069 --> 00:08:59.739
And it's just like,
we as comedians,

00:08:59.739 --> 00:09:02.341
me as a comedian
I feel a big debt

00:09:02.341 --> 00:09:05.945
to this world right now
to bring some joy.

00:09:05.945 --> 00:09:08.780
Like, it's not all hopeless,
I promise.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:09.882
[ Applause ]
And I know.

00:09:09.882 --> 00:09:12.818
Listen, listen,
I know that you're like,

00:09:12.818 --> 00:09:15.487
"Oh, bitch, you're rich,
and blah, blah, blah,

00:09:15.487 --> 00:09:17.356
and you can't tell me,
blah, blah, blah."

00:09:17.356 --> 00:09:19.692
Yes, I can.

00:09:19.692 --> 00:09:20.826
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:20.826 --> 00:09:22.594
Yes, I can.

00:09:22.594 --> 00:09:24.630
We have to have hope.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.732
You have to believe
in yourself

00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:28.868
and in the people that you --

00:09:28.868 --> 00:09:33.405
We have to work together
and we have to be uncomfortable.

00:09:33.405 --> 00:09:34.473
That is the problem.

00:09:34.473 --> 00:09:35.775
We're not doing anything

00:09:35.775 --> 00:09:38.644
because we're complacent
and we're comfortable.

00:09:38.644 --> 00:09:41.713
We have to be uncomfortable
to fix some of this [bleep].

00:09:41.713 --> 00:09:42.948
You know what I'm saying?

00:09:42.948 --> 00:09:45.850
[ Cheers and applause ]
Real talk.

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:48.787
Get your ass up and vote.

00:09:48.787 --> 00:09:50.756
Only a third of the country
is voting.

00:09:50.756 --> 00:09:54.826
If 60 people are in the room

00:09:54.826 --> 00:09:59.265
and only 20 are ordering food,
guess what you gonna eat?

00:09:59.265 --> 00:10:02.301
What the 20 people ordered!

00:10:02.301 --> 00:10:05.404
Pick your [bleep] menu up!

00:10:05.404 --> 00:10:09.275
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:12.644 --> 00:10:16.515
-Well, I disagree
with everything she just said.

00:10:16.515 --> 00:10:17.983
Another really cool thing,

00:10:17.983 --> 00:10:19.551
before you go, everybody here
in the audience tonight --

00:10:19.551 --> 00:10:20.686
talking about bringing joy --

00:10:20.686 --> 00:10:22.287
is going to get a copy
of Leslie's book.

00:10:22.287 --> 00:10:24.790
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yes!

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:26.658
-We'll be right back
with Judd Apatow.

00:10:26.658 --> 00:10:27.760
There's nobody like her!

